<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # All the Pencils! – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> Hold Up Ten Fingers!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> All the Pencils!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Engage in interactive labeling activities.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write simple words with teacher support (e.g., 'desk' or 'book'). Can engage in interactive labeling activities by identifying and labeling objects in shared drawings (e.g., writing labels under numbers supported with pictures representing those numbers). Can practice writing numbers, with model guidance (e.g., writing the numbers from one to ten following a number chart with labels).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; background-color: #f2f2f2;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</b></th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; background-color: #f2f2f2;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</b></th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; background-color: #f2f2f2;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</b></th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Count Your Fingers!" activity. Students hold up their hands and count fingers from one to ten, using TPR. Teacher prompts: "Show me one finger!" "Show me five fingers!" "Count your fingers!" (Practices /f/ in 'fingers' and /k/ in 'count', counting aloud, quantity + nouns).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models writing numbers 1-3 on the board or a large chart, explicitly showing stroke order. "Look! This is number one. Down, down, down." The teacher also models tracing the word "one" next to the number, emphasizing clear letter formation for Kinder students. "This is how we write 'o-n-e', one."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of numbers and the concept of writing/labeling.
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to number 3) "What number is this?" (Expected: "Three!")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a picture of 2 pencils) "How many pencils? Can we write 'two'?" (Expected: "Two! Yes!")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Hold Up Ten Fingers!")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, colorful poster titled "My Classroom Supplies!" featuring numbered images of various school items (e.g., 1. a red pencil, 2. two yellow crayons, 3. three blue books, etc., up to ten blocks). Each image has a blank line for labeling. A word bank with "pencil," "crayon," "book," "marker," "glue," "block" and numbers 1-10 (both numeral and word form) is visible at the bottom. This reinforces numbers (1-10) and vocabulary (pencil, book, crayon, marker, glue, block).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students recognize and understand the visual representation of quantity and objects, preparing them to write corresponding labels.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher points to images and asks:
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What number is this?" (Pointing to number 4)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How many crayons do you see?" (Pointing to an image of crayons) (Answering quantity questions)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is this number five?" (Pointing to number 5).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice writing numbers (1-10) and simple object words (pencil, book, crayon, marker, glue, block) in preparation for interactive labeling. Grammar: Quantity + nouns.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Tracing Numbers (Controlled practice):</b> Students receive a worksheet with large, dotted outlines of numbers 1-10. They trace each number multiple times, saying the number aloud as they trace. The teacher circulates, providing individual guidance on stroke formation. This practices phonemic awareness of number sounds (1-10).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Picture-Word Match and Trace (Semi-controlled):</b> Students get another worksheet with pictures of school supplies (e.g., 1 pencil, 2 books, 3 crayons, etc.) next to a column of dotted words (e.g., "pencil," "book," "crayon"). Students first draw a line to match the picture to the correct word, then trace the word. This reinforces vocabulary and letter sounds (F, G, H, I, J for words like Finger, Glue, Hand, Ink, Juice if applicable from other lessons, but primarily school supplies here).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Guided Labeling (Pair Interaction/Peer Check):</b> Students work in pairs with a shared drawing of a classroom scene featuring objects to be labeled (e.g., a table with 3 books, a shelf with 5 blocks, 1 big crayon). Each object has a numbered blank line. The teacher provides a word bank for the labels (e.g., "book," "block," "crayon," "pencil") and numbers (1-10). Students take turns identifying an object, stating its quantity, and then writing the number and the word label on the line (e.g., "3 books"). They check each other's writing for basic legibility and correctness. (Encouraging turn-taking and active listening).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Hold Up Ten Fingers!")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Interactive "My School Bag" labeling activity. Each student receives a drawing of an open school bag with different school supplies inside (e.g., 4 pencils, 1 book, 2 markers, 6 crayons, 1 glue). Each item has a small circle with a number (1-5) and a blank line next to it. They also have a personal "number chart" with numbers 1-10 and a simplified word bank for the items (e.g., "pencil," "book," "marker," "crayon," "glue").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students identify each item and count the quantity of each.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Using their number chart and word bank as a guide, they write the correct number (e.g., '4') and the corresponding word (e.g., 'pencils') on the line next to each item in their drawing. (e.g., "4 pencils," "1 book").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher encourages them to use their fingers to count and trace letters/numbers in the air before writing.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After labeling, students can color their "My School Bag" picture.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in writing numbers and simple words, accurate identification and labeling of objects. This directly applies "Quantity + nouns" and the vocabulary learned.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "My School Bag" labeling activity, focusing on individual student effort and participation. A simple rubric for the completed labeling worksheet will assess accuracy and legibility.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their "My School Bag" labeled drawing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can write numbers 1-10 (legibility and correct formation).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can write simple words like 'pencil', 'book', 'crayon', 'marker', 'glue', 'block' (legibility and correct spelling with support).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correctly identifies and labels objects in the drawing with the right number and word.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Engages in the labeling activity with teacher support.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students show their labeled drawing to the teacher and point to one labeled item, saying "I can write 'four pencils'!" (or similar). The teacher can ask:
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you write number 7?" (Students write on a small whiteboard or paper).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you write 'book'?" (Students write on a small whiteboard or paper).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one number you can write all by yourself?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one school supply word you like to write?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 4px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this writing lesson for Kinder students? The visual supports and tracing activities seemed highly effective in building confidence.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for next time? Some students still struggle with letter formation for words like 'marker' or 'crayon'. More direct modeling and guided tracing of these specific words might be beneficial. Maybe incorporate a letter sound review for initial letters (M for marker, C for crayon) linked to writing practice.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration) will build on the writing foundation by having students create a simple inventory list or a short sentence about their favorite school supplies, consolidating their ability to write numbers and object names. This will integrate the 21st Century Skills Project by applying their writing skills to a practical task.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>